node_modules

# Databases are build products, not source. The collector's is 7 GB; the record is rebuilt by `npm run servicedb`.
data/*.db
data/*.db-*

# Secrets and session material.
.env
.env.bak.*
data/telegram.session

# Generated output and run artifacts.
site/
data/reports/
*.log

# Small derived data that IS worth versioning, because it is how claims are checked and reproduced:
!data/x-mute.json

# Caller lists. Local operating files, never published: they name identifiable X accounts and
# Telegram channels alongside our own unverified characterisations of what those people were
# doing ("caller network seen posting the CA at or just before launch"). Publishing an
# unverified adverse claim about a named person is the exact thing every published sentence on
# this site is written to avoid, and these belong to the trading thesis, which is dead.
kols.txt
channels.txt
channels.candidates.txt
data/x-mute.json

# macOS.
.DS_Store

# Held back from the public repository, deliberately and temporarily. HANDOFF.md is a live
# working document: it currently describes the unseeded cloud collector and the record build
# that is failing silently, which is a map of an open weakness rather than a description of
# method. Publish it once the collector is seeded and that build works. README.md,
# ASSUMPTIONS.md and DEPLOY.md carry everything a reader needs in the meantime.
HANDOFF.md


# Grant application drafts. Working documents with figures that go stale, and negotiating
# positions that should not be public while they are being negotiated.
.grants/

# Runtime traces from the launchd jobs: stderr, and the freshness probe's alert-suppression state. data/*.log was
# already covered by the rule above; these were not, and the hourly probe writes them.
data/*.err
data/*.state
data/UNDELIVERED-ALERT.txt

# Written by scripts/deposit.py to describe each DOI deposit; regenerated per deposit, not source.
data/_README.md

# Captured launch images are evidence that travels separately, never in the repository or the archive.
data/images/
data/images.err

# Client working files. A scoped engagement workspace (SCOPE.md, src/engagement.ts) is a matter's evidence file:
# it names a client's subject, its parties and their funders. It must never reach the public remote AND must never
# reach production - `railway up` deploys the WORKING TREE, so an ignored file is the only kind that is safe to
# have in this checkout at all.
#
# The rule above it is not enough and that was checked, not assumed: `data/*.db` does not cross a directory
# separator, so `data/engagements/M-2026-001.db` was reported untracked by `git status` and would have been swept
# up by the `git add -A` that has already taken the site down once.
data/engagements/
*.engagement.db

# The labelled validation set. 664 named mints, each carrying label "manufactured" with a family-level
# justification applied as a per-token verdict. It is a legitimate internal instrument - it is how `npm run labels`
# checks the clean criteria against evidence the criteria never look at - and it was committed and public from
# 2026-09-07. But "manufactured" is a claim about PURPOSE, and it is the exact word removed from the site on
# 2026-09-10 for that reason: we observe supply shares, buyer counts and timings, never purposes. A public file
# asserting it against 664 identifiable mints says what the site refuses to say. `labels.ts` regenerates it with
# `npm run labels -- --build` and exits with that instruction if it is absent, so nothing breaks.
#
# THE BETTER END STATE, not done here because it is a publication decision: republish it with the label naming the
# OBSERVATION rather than the conclusion - ticker-family identity burning - which would restore the transparency
# that publishing a validation set is for, without the purpose claim.
data/labels.json

# Hand-traced operator clusters: 50 named wallet addresses under a funder, with our own note characterising what
# that group did. Committed in the first public commit (2026-09-07) and removed 2026-09-12 for the same reason
# kols.txt and channels.txt are held back above: it is an unverified adverse characterisation of identifiable
# parties, written before we learned that trading terminals fund their users from one address exactly as a wallet
# farm funds its own. `clusters.ts` reads it only as a local seed and guards with existsSync, so nothing breaks.
data/operator-clusters.json
